WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
*Second Thought Intro*

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:08.350
If you are like me spinning around in a circle for just a few seconds is enough to render you

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:13.809
uncomfortably dizzy. Now imagine if you had to spend every second of every day spinning at a rate of

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:20.649
460 meters per second or roughly 1000 miles per hour. Well, provided you're standing near the equator,

00:00:20.649 --> 00:00:27.069
you kind of already do. the Earth makes one full rotation every 23 hours and 56 minutes

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:33.759
and it's circumference is Roughly 40,075 kilometers, which gives the surface of our home planet a rotational speed of

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.220
460 meters per second around the equator.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:43.119
Besides giving us what we know as our day and night cycle, the planet's rotation has profound effects on the conditions of Earth.

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:46.479
What would happen if that rotation suddenly stopped?

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:52.329
If we assume that some unknown force stopped the Earth instantly, the immediate effect would be disastrous.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:58.570
The effect would be similar to what you'd experience if you hit an immovable barrier on the highway while not wearing a seat belt.

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:01.599
Except instead of traveling at 70 miles an hour,

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:05.799
you'd be going a thousand miles an hour. The earth itself would stop spinning,

00:01:05.799 --> 00:01:12.159
but the atmosphere, liquids, and most objects around the world would be launched sideways at 460 meters per second.

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:16.510
Buildings would be leveled by winds exceeding the speed of atomic blast shockwaves.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:24.489
Humans, animals, and cars would become billions of individual ballistic missiles. The momentum and sudden lack of gravity near the equator would create enormous

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:30.850
kilometers high tsunamis that could sweep across land masses in a matter of minutes as the world's oceans rush towards the poles.

00:01:31.159 --> 00:01:38.709
Any area that is lucky enough to survive the destructive winds and massive tsunamis would likely be stripped bare by more gradual erosion from continued winds

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:41.440
destroying animal habitats, homes, and farmland.

00:01:41.869 --> 00:01:46.869
The world would suffer from massive droughts and with most or all of the water gone or far away,

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:50.400
there would be no way to support life for an extended period of time.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:56.680
The immediate aftermath of this planetary braking is severe and would likely put every species on Earth, including

00:01:56.990 --> 00:02:00.610
humans on the critically endangered list, if not extinct altogether.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.880
But what about the long-term effects of a non-spinning Earth?

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:09.369
There would be many less destructive effects to the change, and some serious ones that might not be obvious at first

00:02:10.099 --> 00:02:12.968
For example, days would last much, much longer

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:16.299
Right now it takes the Earth about 24 hours

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:23.560
to spin on its axis, marking one full day. If the planet stopped spinning gradually, say, over the course of several billion years

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:31.100
it would eventually reach a point where it would be considered sun synchronous, meaning one rotation of the Earth would take 365 days.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:37.740
Similar to tidal locking, this state would cause every spot on Earth to exist in permanent day or permanent night.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:44.320
The oceans would boil, or evaporate entirely on the day side of the planet and freeze on the night side, making life

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:46.700
exceptionally difficult, if not impossible

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:50.320
except in the slim twilight regions between the day and night sides.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:57.120
If instead the Earth reached a point where it had stopped spinning entirely, you would experience half a year of sunlight and half a year of

00:02:57.220 --> 00:03:00.620
darkness, which might allow for slightly more livable conditions,

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:06.520
but would likely ruin agriculture and plant growth cycles as there would be no real transition between seasons.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:12.460
One very gradual change would be the planets change from its current globe shape to a more perfect sphere.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:16.840
Right now due to the Earth's rotation, there is a slight bulge around the equator,

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.580
making the Earth more of a deflated ball than a sphere.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:26.020
Because of this bulge, the area around the equator holds more of the world's ocean water. As the Earth shifted towards a more

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:31.780
spherical shape, this water would flood surrounding areas as it migrates towards the poles where gravity is the strongest.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.660
This would create two enormous polar oceans and leave one giant continent

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:38.270
that would wrap around the equator

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:44.260
If you lived in this polar ocean world you could walk around the entire Earth without ever having to cross a body of water.

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:50.200
Another gradual but more lethal change would be the slow dissipation of the Earth's magnetic field.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:55.140
When the planet spins, molten metal from the core rises, cools, and sinks, back down,

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:58.040
creating convection currents and generating magnetic energy.

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:03.519
If the Earth stopped spinning, this process would grind to a halt and the planet's magnetic field would fade away,

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:08.480
leaving the surface to be bombarded with cosmic radiation and lethal rays from the sun.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:14.220
Years of constant baking from radiation would render the Earth rocky and lifeless, much like our sister planet Mars,

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:21.130
which once had a magnetosphere of its own. When Mars lost its magnetic field, the sun slowly stripped away the planet's atmosphere,

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:24.820
scorching the surface and leaving the barren red planet we have today.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:30.080
So, the Earth's rotation is really the only thing standing between us and planet-wide disaster.

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:37.649
Luckily, there's not really any chance of that spin suddenly coming to a halt. Sure, the planet will slowly stop spinning,

00:04:37.689 --> 00:04:40.499
but it'll be over the course of billions of years and

00:04:40.659 --> 00:04:47.639
humanity will most likely be extinct or long gone by then, and as far as we know there's nothing that could stop the Earth's rotation.

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:54.700
Immediately with the exception of maybe another planet colliding with ours, but in that case, we'd be dead either way.

